 ## Operating characteristics
 **Time and Memory Complexity:**
-Bilinear interpolation requires a constant number of arithmetic operations (4 multiplications, 4 additions/subtractions, and 2 floor operations) and 4 memory accesses per query, regardless of the grid size. The time complexity is $O(1)$ per sample [S5]. Memory complexity is proportional to the size of the grid $W \times H$ [S8, unofficial].
+Bilinear interpolation requires a constant number of arithmetic operations (8 multiplications, 4 additions/subtractions) and memory accesses (4 samples) per query, regardless of grid size [S5]. The memory complexity is $O(W \cdot H)$ for storing the grid [S8, unofficial].
 
 **Measured Performance:**
-Performance depends heavily on hardware support. Modern GPUs have dedicated hardware units for bilinear texture sampling, making it extremely fast [S9, unofficial]. In software implementations, SIMD optimizations can significantly accelerate the process [S1].
+Performance is highly dependent on hardware support. Modern GPUs provide dedicated bilinear filtering instructions (e.g., `LRP` in some architectures, or texture sampling units) which execute in a single cycle per fragment when accessed via texture caches [S9, unofficial]. In software implementations, SIMD optimizations can significantly accelerate the process [S1].
 
 **Scaling:**
-The method scales linearly with the number of samples (pixels/voxels) but is independent of the grid resolution in terms of per-sample cost. However, higher resolution grids may lead to increased cache misses if the access pattern is not coherent [S1].
+The method scales linearly with the number of query points. It is independent of the total grid size, making it suitable for large volumes where only local samples are needed [S5].
 
 **Strengths and Limitations:**
-*   **Strengths:** Simple to implement, computationally efficient, provides smoother results than nearest-neighbor interpolation [S5].
-*   **Limitations:** $C^0$ continuity only, leading to discontinuous gradients at cell boundaries [S5]. Can introduce blurring artifacts, especially when downscaling [S5]. Not suitable for high-frequency details or sharp edges without additional techniques (e.g., anisotropic filtering) [S17, unofficial].
+*   **Strengths:** Computationally efficient; provides smoother results than nearest neighbor; easy to implement; supports differentiable rendering (gradients are well-defined) [S7, S10, unofficial].
+*   **Limitations:** $C^0$ continuity only; prone to blurring high-frequency details; suffers from aliasing if the sampling rate is insufficient (Nyquist criterion not met) [S5].
 
 **When to Use:**
-Use bilinear interpolation when sampling 2D textures, voxel faces, or when computational efficiency is critical and high-frequency detail preservation is not the primary concern [S5, S16].
+Use bilinear interpolation for general-purpose volume rendering where performance is critical and high-frequency detail preservation is not the primary goal. It is ideal for texture mapping, face value computation in ray casting, and as a base for more complex filters [S6, unofficial][S16].
 
 **When Not to Use:**
-Avoid when $C^1$ continuity is required (use cubic or spline interpolation) or when sampling 3D volumes where trilinear or higher-order methods are more appropriate [S5][S12, unofficial].
+Avoid when $C^1$ continuity is required (use cubic or spline interpolation) or when preserving sharp edges is critical without post-processing (use edge-aware methods) [S17, unofficial].
 
 ## Background
-Bilinear interpolation is a fundamental technique in computer graphics and image processing, extending the concept of linear interpolation to two dimensions [S5]. It has been widely used for tasks such as image resizing, texture mapping, and volume rendering [S5]. The method is often implemented in hardware via texture sampling units in GPUs [S9, unofficial]. In the context of volume rendering, it is used to sample values on voxel faces or to handle 2D attributes associated with 3D primitives [S6, unofficial][S16].
+Bilinear interpolation is a fundamental technique in computer graphics and image processing. It was widely adopted in early volume rendering systems for its simplicity and efficiency [S5]. The method is often used in conjunction with ray casting to sample density and color values from volume textures [S6, unofficial]. In the context of Fourier space reconstruction, bilinear interpolation is used to retrieve values from polar grids to Cartesian grids [S11, unofficial]. It is also a core component in differentiable rendering pipelines, such as those used in 3D Gaussian Splatting variants (e.g., SVGS) to model spatially varying colors [S15].
 
